  "title": "Bhotekoshi glacier collapse and debris flood, Nepal (August 2026)",
  "event_date": "2026-08-26",
  "story": "On the morning of 26 August 2026, rock and glacier ice broke off Langtang Lirung and briefly dammed the river. The flood that followed swept down the Bhote Koshi through Rasuwagadhi, Timure and Syabrubesi, killing more than 1,400 people",
  "method": "Coherence measures how similar the ground's radar fingerprint is between two passes 12 days apart. Ground that was ripped up, buried or flooded in between loses coherence, so a drop compared with the usual coherence marks where the flood went. Backscatter before and after, averaged to 50 m, shows how the surface changed",
  "limitations": "Passes are 12 days apart, so the radar brackets the collapse (19 and 31 August) rather than catching it. Coherence is at 80 m, so narrow channels blur, and steep slopes facing away from the radar have gaps",
